/* Phase 12 English content: reviewed lessons 1-39. */
window.AppNhoPhase12EN = {"lessons":{"1":{"title":"Garment Factory Organizational Structure","cat":"Organization and Management Systems","overview":"A garment factory’s organizational structure should be designed around decision flows and operational accountability. Common functions include executive management, production, industrial engineering (IE), quality assurance (QA), technical, merchandising, planning, warehouse, human resources, finance and maintenance. Smaller factories may combine functions, but they must not eliminate essential controls.","method":"Map the current structure, then create a responsibility table for each key decision: who proposes, checks, approves, executes and receives the information. Prioritize high-risk decision points such as sample approval, production order release, specification changes, line stoppage, release of nonconforming goods and shipment authorization.","caution":"A reporting hierarchy does not, by itself, establish approval authority. For transactions involving financial or quality risks, one person should not create the data, approve it and independently confirm the result.","deep":"During a review, select a real scenario—for example, a shade variation detected before cutting. Trace the process from the person who identifies the issue to the person authorized to hold the lot, the person who assesses its impact, the person who decides its disposition and the person who informs the customer. If staff have to make repeated calls to find the decision-maker, the structure has a missing link. For each position, document its reporting line, management scope, signing authority, authority to stop work and designated deputy during absences. Check that the organization chart is consistent with job descriptions, the RACI matrix and system access rights. One person may cover more than one function, but appropriate independent review must remain in place. Whenever the structure changes, update the delegation-of-authority list, forms, SOPs and escalation directory together. Communicate the effective date so that two versions are not used in parallel.","applied":"Choose a practical decision, such as stopping a sewing line because of a quality defect, and trace it from the person who detects the issue to the person authorized to stop the line, the person who confirms the cause and the person who authorizes restart. If several departments are involved but no single owner is accountable, response time will increase. Test the organization chart against scenarios such as style changes, material shortages, accidents, labor complaints and machine breakdowns. Each scenario should have a clearly identified decision-maker, executor and reporting route.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Garment Factory Organizational Structure\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Garment Factory Organizational Structure</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Garment Factory Organizational Structure</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Workflow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Decision Flow</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Approval Authority</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Interface Review</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Evidence, accountable owner and applicable document revision must be aligned.</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Align evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":1},"2":{"title":"Departmental Roles and Responsibilities","cat":"Organization and Management Systems","overview":"Departmental roles should be defined through measurable outputs rather than broad lists of duties. Production is responsible for executing the plan; QA verifies conformity; IE analyzes methods and capacity; planning coordinates schedules; the warehouse controls materials; and finance records costs in accordance with company policy.","method":"Prepare a charter for each department that defines its scope, inputs, outputs, decision rights, KPIs, records and handover points. Hold cross-functional meetings around specific decisions, with a designated chair, pre-circulated data and deadlines for closing actions.","caution":"Do not assign sole responsibility for quality to QA or sole responsibility for on-time delivery to planning. Quality and delivery depend on several functions, and each function must own its part of the process.","deep":"Consider the production order release process. Planning confirms the schedule and capacity; merchandising confirms customer requirements; the warehouse confirms material availability; technical confirms the documentation; QA confirms inspection criteria; and production confirms labor and equipment readiness. An order is ready only when mandatory conditions are supported by evidence—not merely because a meeting has ended. Assign one accountable owner to each output, identify the teams that execute the work and specify who must be consulted. Departmental KPIs should include balancing measures: output alongside quality, delivery alongside schedule stability, and cost savings alongside compliance and safety. When priorities conflict, apply an approved prioritization rule rather than renegotiating the decision on each shift. Periodically review handover points against actual records to identify work that has been missed or duplicated.","applied":"When building a responsibility matrix, use a cross-functional process such as bulk material approval or the handling of nonconforming goods. List the inputs, decisions, records and handover points. Then ask each department what information it receives, how quickly it must respond and where it has the authority to reject or stop the process. Gaps often occur between merchandising, technical, purchasing, the warehouse, QA and production. A matrix is useful only when employees understand it and actual records reflect the assigned responsibilities.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Departmental Roles and Responsibilities\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Departmental Roles and Responsibilities</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Departmental Roles and Responsibilities</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Workflow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Handover Points</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Decision Authority</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Cross-functional Outcome</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Evidence, accountable owner and applicable document revision must be aligned.</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Align evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":2},"3":{"title":"Process Management and Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs)","cat":"Organization and Management Systems","overview":"A process describes a sequence of activities and the relationships between roles, while an SOP explains how to perform a specific task. The two documents should be linked, but neither replaces the other. A good SOP enables trained employees to work consistently and tells them what to do when conditions are abnormal.","method":"Observe the work on the factory floor. Identify inputs and outputs, the person performing the task, tools, sequence, acceptance criteria, forms and exception-handling steps. Test the document with representative users before approval. Control the document code, revision, effective date and withdrawal of obsolete copies.","caution":"Do not write an SOP from memory alone or copy another factory’s document without validation. Remove obsolete instructions from workstations once a revised version becomes effective.","deep":"An in-line inspection SOP should specify the reference sample, suitable measuring tools, measurement points, sample quantity under the control plan and the method for recording results. When a deviation is found, the operator must know where to stop, how to identify affected goods, whom to contact and what conditions must be met before work resumes. Each step should use an observable action verb such as inspect, measure, compare, record, segregate or verify. Avoid vague instructions such as “check carefully.” Test the SOP by asking a newly trained employee to perform the task without further explanation from its author. Record any step that causes confusion, requires an unavailable tool or lacks the data needed to complete a form. After release, verify compliance through observation and records. If actual practice differs from the document, investigate the cause before revising the SOP or arranging retraining. Retain the change history and distribution list so that the applicable revision can be traced.","applied":"An SOP should begin by defining its scope and input conditions, followed by the work steps, control points, output records, responsibilities and exception-handling requirements. Before release, test it with the employees who perform the task and confirm that they can complete the process without asking for essential information. When a machine, software application, form or customer requirement changes, the process owner should assess the impact and update the revision. Withdraw obsolete copies from points of use to prevent two different methods from being followed at the same time.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Process Management and SOPs\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Process Management and SOPs</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Process Management and SOPs</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Workflow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Draft the SOP</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Trial on the Factory Floor</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Revision Control</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Evidence, accountable owner and applicable document revision must be aligned.</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Align evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":3},"4":{"title":"RACI Matrix and Delegation of Authority","cat":"Organization and Management Systems","overview":"RACI clarifies four roles for a task: Responsible (does the work), Accountable (owns the final outcome), Consulted (provides input) and Informed (receives updates). The matrix is useful when a decision crosses departments, but it has value only when it is tied to a specific output.","method":"List the decisions or deliverables in each process and assign roles rather than relying only on individual names. Review every row. A task should normally have one A; the R must have the resources and data access needed to perform the work. Stop-work authority for safety or quality must be defined separately.","caution":"Do not use RACI as a substitute for legal requirements, financial delegation limits, technical approval rules or the authority to reject nonconforming products.","deep":"For a material change, technical may assess sewability, QA may assess inspection criteria, merchandising may communicate with the customer, the warehouse may check the replacement lot and production may confirm the impact on operations. However, the person authorized to give final approval must be identified in accordance with the contract and change-control procedure. When reviewing the matrix, look for three common failures: no A, leaving the decision unresolved; multiple As, allowing departments to shift responsibility; or an R who lacks access to the sample, data or equipment required. Distinguish Consulted from Informed: a consulted party must have an opportunity to respond before the decision, while an informed party receives the outcome after it is finalized. Test the matrix against a real abnormal situation and measure how long it takes to identify the correct decision-maker. Update it when the organization, customer, process or approval system changes.","applied":"Build the RACI matrix around individual decisions or outputs, not simply department names. Each row should have one Accountable owner; Responsible identifies the person doing the work, while Consulted and Informed are assigned according to their required involvement. Multiple A assignments or the absence of an R can delay a process. Test the matrix against a specification change, an expense approval outside the budget and an accident response. Authority must be consistent with the company’s signing rules and applicable legal limits.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"RACI Matrix and Delegation of Authority\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>RACI Matrix and Delegation of Authority</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">RACI Matrix and Delegation of Authority</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Workflow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">RACI by Role</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Authority Check</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Scenario Test</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Evidence, accountable owner and applicable document revision must be aligned.</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Align evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":4},"5":{"title":"Workforce Planning and Headcount Estimation","cat":"Workforce and Labor Capability","overview":"Headcount planning converts production demand into required labor minutes and then into people, based on available working time and planned efficiency. It is a capacity-planning exercise involving skills and work schedules, not a simple division of output by the number of employees currently available.","method":"Calculate total standard minutes = planned output × approved SAM. Divide this by the available working minutes per person for the period and the planned efficiency to estimate direct labor. Then review skill requirements, shifts, absence, trainees, special operations and indirect labor.","caution":"Do not use an unapproved SAM or an efficiency target without a sound basis. Do not count breaks or allowances twice if they have already been accounted for in the denominator or calculation method.","deep":"Assume an order requires 1,200 pieces per day, the SAM is 18 minutes, each employee has 420 productive minutes and planned efficiency is 60%. The calculated requirement is 1,200 × 18 / (420 × 0.60), or approximately 85.7 direct operators. This is an aggregate estimate based on minutes; it does not prove that the line has enough skills at bottleneck operations. Next, allocate standard minutes by operation, identify special-machine positions, determine who can operate multiple machines and allow for absence coverage. Prepare at least a base scenario and a risk scenario, with explicit assumptions for efficiency, style changeover time and the learning curve. Compare the headcount plan with the skill matrix and training schedule before committing to a delivery date. Once production starts, compare actual manpower, paid minutes, good output and overtime with the original assumptions. Update the model rather than applying one fixed efficiency rate to every style.","applied":"Headcount planning should combine order demand, approved SAM, work schedules, skills, absence rates, training time and machine capacity. A total headcount calculation does not reveal whether the skill mix can cover bottleneck operations. Plan by skill group and shift, then cross-check against the skill matrix and leave schedule. For a new order, model overtime, labor transfers or recruitment and assess the impact on cost, quality, safety and ramp-up time.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Workforce Planning and Headcount Estimation\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Workforce Planning and Headcount Estimation</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Workforce Planning and Headcount Estimation</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Workflow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Available Minutes</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Headcount Conversion</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Review Skills and Shifts</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Evidence, accountable owner and applicable document revision must be aligned.</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Align evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":5},"6":{"title":"Recruitment and New-Employee Onboarding","cat":"Workforce and Labor Capability","overview":"Recruitment should start with the work to be performed and the capabilities required. Onboarding helps new employees understand the workplace, its risks and its working methods before they are assigned independent duties. Attendance records from training do not replace an assessment of practical competence.","method":"Set recruitment criteria for each position, describe the required operations and safety capabilities, and identify the assessors. During onboarding, cover workplace rules, emergency exits, incident reporting, PPE, quality, confidentiality, timekeeping and the assigned trainer. Assess practical performance before authorizing independent work.","caution":"Do not assign a machine or high-risk operation to an employee who has not received the required instruction and had their competence confirmed under factory rules. Do not use probationary employment to bypass applicable labor obligations.","deep":"A new sewing operator should be introduced to the correct machine model, its guards, the stop procedure, broken-needle handling, permitted needle and thread types and the quality criteria for the operation. The trainer demonstrates the task; the trainee practices under supervision and is then assessed using an actual sample and an observation checklist. If the trainee does not meet the standard, record the skills requiring further practice and the person responsible for coaching. Do not mark the employee as competent simply because a set number of training days has passed. Track time to competence, early-stage defects, absence and early turnover to assess recruitment and onboarding quality. Review these data by job and recruitment source, and avoid drawing conclusions from isolated cases. Training records should include the date, content, trainer, result and scope of work the employee is authorized to perform.","applied":"Separate induction on workplace rules from confirmation of job competence. Before assigning an employee to an operation, provide instruction on safety, hazard recognition, incident reporting, quality rules and the basic tasks of the position. Trainers need defined assessment criteria and must record practical results rather than merely signing an attendance sheet. After an appropriate period on the job, the direct supervisor should review defect rates, support needs and feedback from the new employee to improve training or job placement.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Recruitment and New-Employee Onboarding\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Recruitment and New-Employee Onboarding</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Recruitment and New-Employee Onboarding</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Workflow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Safety Induction</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Supervised Practice</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Competence Assessment</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Evidence, accountable owner and applicable document revision must be aligned.</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Align evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":6},"7":{"title":"Skills Matrix and Multi-Skilling","cat":"Workforce and Labor Capability","overview":"A skills matrix records each employee’s demonstrated capability by operation or skill group. It supports workforce allocation, training plans and absence coverage. It is reliable only when each proficiency level has observable criteria and the records are kept up to date.","method":"Define proficiency levels in terms of observable behavior: not yet trained; trained and working under supervision; able to work independently to the required standard; and able to train others. Verify competence through product samples, quality, time, safety and the ability to handle deviations.","caution":"Do not infer competence from seniority, the number of training sessions attended or previous machine experience. Do not use the matrix to rank individuals unless the assessment criteria have been agreed.","deep":"For a T-shirt collar attaching operation, criteria may include correct component preparation, control of stretch, alignment of matching points, even stitching, freedom from puckering or twisting and compliance with the approved measurement. The assessor should observe several representative cycles, inspect the output and record the verification date. An operator who is competent on single jersey may not yet be competent on rib fabric with different recovery characteristics. The skill scope should therefore identify the material, machine or product group when these differences are significant. Use the matrix to identify operations that depend on only one qualified person, then plan cross-training and reassessment. When the machine, folder, method or customer standard changes, consider reconfirming competence. Retain assessment history so that the current matrix can be traced to its supporting evidence.","applied":"To make the matrix reflect actual capability, define criteria for each level: understanding the process, working under supervision, meeting the quality standard independently and being able to train others. Evidence may include practical samples, checklist-based observation, quality results and assessor confirmation. A skill that was previously approved may need reassessment after a style or machine change. Use the matrix to plan remedial training for high-risk operations and reduce dependence on a small number of employees with scarce skills.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Skills Matrix and Multi-Skilling\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Skills Matrix and Multi-Skilling</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Skills Matrix and Multi-Skilling</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Workflow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Observable Criteria</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Practical Assessment</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Training Plan</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Evidence, accountable owner and applicable document revision must be aligned.</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Align evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":7},"8":{"title":"Timekeeping and Working-Time Management","cat":"Workforce and Labor Capability","overview":"Timekeeping data supports payroll, working-time management, productivity analysis and overtime control. Attendance time, payable working time, breaks, absence, overtime and time adjustments must be distinguished; these data fields are not interchangeable.","method":"Synchronize shift schedules, employee IDs, time clocks and break rules. Reconcile clock-in and clock-out records against rosters, leave requests, transfers and supervisor approvals before closing the period. Every adjustment must have a reason, an approver and an audit trail.","caution":"Limits on working hours, rest periods and record retention depend on local law and applicable agreements. Do not create rules that exceed legal limits or share personal data beyond legitimate work requirements.","deep":"A missing clock-out should not automatically be treated as proof that an employee worked the full shift. HR should verify the record with the employee and supervisor, consult CCTV or other records only where policy permits, and retain the reason for any adjustment. In reporting, distinguish attendance minutes from productive minutes used in an efficiency calculation. If all attendance time is used as the denominator, disclose how breaks, meetings, training and waiting time are treated. Before payroll is closed, prepare an exception list covering missed punches, duplicate records, unapproved overtime, shift changes and leave that has not been updated. Restrict editing rights by role. The log should retain the before-and-after values, timestamp and user who made the change. When a dispute arises, the payslip should be traceable back to the shift schedule and original clock records.","applied":"The timekeeping system should define recording points, overnight shifts, overtime, meal and rest breaks, lateness, data corrections and approval authority. Reconcile clock records with shift schedules and payroll in a controlled cycle. Every manual adjustment must include a reason and the identity of the person making it. When analyzing absence or overtime, review patterns by department, shift and peak period to identify organizational causes while complying with labor requirements in the applicable market.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Timekeeping and Working-Time Management\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Timekeeping and Working-Time Management</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Timekeeping and Working-Time Management</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Workflow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Exception Reconciliation</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Adjustment Approval</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Period Close and Audit Trail</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Evidence, accountable owner and applicable document revision must be aligned.</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Align evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":8},"9":{"title":"Wages and Piece-Rate Compensation","cat":"Workforce and Labor Capability","overview":"A compensation system may combine time-based wages, piece-rate pay, allowances and incentives, subject to company policy and applicable law. For piece-rate pay, the conditions for recognizing output, quality requirements, rates and allocation between operations must be communicated clearly.","method":"Explain the formula with examples. Define what qualifies as accepted output, the source of production records, rates by operation or work group, trainee arrangements, treatment of rework and the reconciliation process. Run a trial using sample-period data before implementation.","caution":"Do not make arbitrary deductions or transfer the risk of system failures or material shortages to employees. All deductions and overtime payments must be handled in accordance with current requirements.","deep":"For an operation paid by piece rate, define whether the unit is a component, subassembly or completed garment. If two employees contribute to the same output, establish and communicate the allocation rule in advance. Production records should include the timestamp, operation code, operator ID and quality status. Record rework separately so that one piece is not counted multiple times and the work involved in repair is not overlooked under the approved policy. Before payroll is processed, reconcile valid output with inter-operation transfer records, working time and the applicable rate table. Test scenarios covering accepted output, held product, rework, a rate change during the period and an employee transferring to another operation. A payslip should explain how the amount was calculated. Employees should know how to request a review when a discrepancy occurs. Do not use productivity as the sole basis for pay decisions when material, machine or planning conditions are outside the employee’s control.","applied":"A piece-rate system must define the unit of output, acceptance conditions, treatment of rework and defects, support operations and the data cut-off time. If output is recorded before QC confirmation, disputes may arise when goods later require repair or rejection. The rate table should have a revision number, style scope and approver. Before changing the system, simulate the effect on earnings across different work groups and check compliance with labor law.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Wages and Piece-Rate Compensation\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Wages and Piece-Rate Compensation</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Wages and Piece-Rate Compensation</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Workflow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Publish the Rate</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Reconcile Production Data</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Test the Payroll Calculation</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Evidence, accountable owner and applicable document revision must be aligned.</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Align evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":9},"10":{"title":"Labor Productivity and Efficiency","cat":"Workforce and Labor Capability","overview":"Labor productivity and efficiency are related but distinct measures. Productivity may be expressed as units per labor hour, while sewing-line efficiency commonly compares the standard minutes earned by good output with actual labor minutes, according to the factory’s definition. Every report must state its numerator and denominator.","method":"Confirm the labor scope, measurement period, SAM revision, accepted output, working hours and treatment of rework. Analyze results by style, line, shift and learning stage, and review the indicators alongside quality, WIP and downtime.","caution":"Do not compare two lines when product mix, SAM, labor scope or time-recording rules differ. Do not use a single KPI to judge an individual employee’s capability.","deep":"Assume a line has 30 operators, 420 minutes per person and produces 500 accepted pieces at a SAM of 20 minutes. Standard minutes earned are 10,000; labor minutes are 12,600; efficiency under this method is 79.4%. If total output is 530 but 30 pieces require repair, using 530 instead of 500 would increase the reported result without reflecting first-pass good output. When analyzing variation, separate the effects of style mix, learning curve, material shortages, machine stoppages, style changeovers, absence and quality defects. Units per labor hour may rise when the factory switches to an easier product even though skills and methods remain unchanged. Use SAM conversion or comparable product groups to make a fair comparison. Retain the formula, data source, cut-off time and standard-time revision in the report so another person can reproduce the calculation.","applied":"When calculating productivity, define whether the output is a completed garment that has passed quality checks or a unit that has completed only one operation. The denominator may be direct labor hours, total labor hours or standard minutes; different definitions produce different results. Reports should show accepted output, labor hours, SAM, rework and downtime together so that higher volume is not rewarded at the expense of passing defects to the next operation. Comparisons between lines should account for product complexity and mix.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Labor Productivity and Efficiency\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Labor Productivity and Efficiency</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Labor Productivity and Efficiency</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Workflow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Standardize SAM and Hours</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Calculate the Measure</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Analyze the Causes</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Evidence, accountable owner and applicable document revision must be aligned.</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Align evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":10},"11":{"title":"Direct Labor Costs","cat":"Finance and Factory Costs","overview":"Direct labor cost covers the labor expenditure that can be assigned to production activities or products under the factory’s costing rules. It may include wages, piece-rate payments, allowances and related employment costs, depending on the accounting policy and the cost scope being used.","method":"Define the cost scope and reporting period first. Reconcile payroll records with attendance and working-time data, then allocate labor costs using a consistent basis such as operation, production order, direct labor hours or approved standard minutes. Document the allocation method and its limitations.","caution":"Do not compare total labor costs across products with different styles, SAM values or levels of complexity unless an appropriate conversion basis is used. Do not classify costs in a way that conflicts with the company’s accounting policy.","deep":"To analyze labor cost per unit, first identify which expenses belong to direct labor and which production quantity will be used as the denominator. When several styles are produced during the period, labor minutes may be allocated by operation or by approved standard minutes, but the chosen method must be stated clearly. Where the system allows, separate regular hours, overtime, training, material waiting time and rework; do not attribute every variance to productivity. A cost increase may result from higher wage rates, more hours worked, lower good output or a change in product mix. Each cause calls for a different response. Reconcile payroll with attendance records and production quantities, and check the reporting period and allocation entries to avoid timing differences. Management reports should show both total labor cost and a normalized measure, such as cost per standard minute or equivalent unit, together with the assumptions used.","applied":"Assign direct labor costs to style codes, operations or production orders using a consistent allocation rule. Distinguish base wages, allowances, overtime, piece-rate payments and related costs in accordance with the applicable accounting policy. When actual cost exceeds the standard, analyze hours, wage rates, productivity, overtime and workforce mix before drawing conclusions; do not assume that low productivity is the cause. Attendance, output and payroll records must be reconcilable.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Direct Labor Costs\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Direct Labor Costs</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Direct Labor Costs</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Define the Cost Scope</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Reconcile Payroll and Hours</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Allocate Using a Defined Basis</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Analyze Variances</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":11},"12":{"title":"Manufacturing Cost Structure","cat":"Finance and Factory Costs","overview":"A manufacturing cost model must define its cost boundaries before its components are added together. A common management model separates direct materials, direct labor and manufacturing overhead. Outsourced processing, packaging, testing and logistics are included according to the scope of the quotation and the company’s accounting policy.","method":"Prepare a cost sheet containing the style code, unit of measure, tech-pack revision, consumption standards, wastage, material prices, SAM, labor rate, overhead rate and quotation sources. Record effective dates, delivery terms, exchange rates and approvers wherever relevant.","caution":"Do not count a cost twice when it is already included in a supplier’s price or in an overhead rate. Do not treat unapproved consumption standards as actual results.","deep":"A cost sheet should allow every figure to be traced to its source: the BOM and consumption standards for materials; quotations and effective dates for prices; approved SAM for labor; and the cost ledger and allocation basis for overhead. For materials, distinguish theoretical consumption from approved wastage; a single wastage percentage should not be applied indiscriminately to every fabric or cutting layout. For labor, record the SAM revision and the labor rate applicable to the period. Overhead may be allocated by labor minutes, machine hours or another basis selected by the company. The basis should not be changed between styles without explanation. When production volume changes, distinguish variable costs from fixed costs to understand movements in unit cost. Before releasing a price, check horizontal and vertical totals, units of measure and possible double counting. Retain the approved calculation so it can be compared with actual production cost.","applied":"The cost structure should show which costs are directly assigned, which are allocated and what allocation basis is used. Fabric and trims often represent a substantial share of garment cost, but labor, wastage, finishing, testing, packaging and factory overhead can also have a significant effect depending on the product. When comparing alternatives, use the same volume, quality requirements, exchange-rate assumptions and input-price date. State all assumptions so an estimated cost is not mistaken for a finalized actual cost.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Manufacturing Cost Structure\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Manufacturing Cost Structure</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Manufacturing Cost Structure</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">BOM and Consumption Standards</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">SAM and Labor Rate</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Overhead and Cost Scope</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Review the Cost Sheet</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":12},"13":{"title":"Manufacturing Overhead and Cost Allocation","cat":"Finance and Factory Costs","overview":"Manufacturing overhead consists of costs that support production but cannot normally be assigned conveniently and directly to an individual product. Depending on company policy, these costs may include depreciation, utilities, maintenance, workshop supervision and shared services.","method":"Define the cost pools, collection period, cost centers and allocation bases. Check whether each allocation base reasonably reflects the activity that drives the cost, and reconcile the total allocated amount with the costs included in each pool.","caution":"Do not select an allocation basis solely because it is easy to calculate if it materially distorts costs between styles. Do not allocate the same expense through more than one cost pool.","deep":"If electricity costs are driven mainly by machines and equipment with high power consumption, machine hours may reflect the cost relationship better than units produced. If workshop supervision is more closely related to labor time, labor minutes may be more appropriate. These are management choices that must be explained and applied consistently; no single allocation basis is suitable for every factory. Prepare a reconciliation for each cost pool showing the opening balance, additions, exclusions and total amount allocated. Check that activity data—such as machine hours, labor minutes or occupied floor area—is complete and relates to the same period. Test the allocation on several styles with different operating characteristics to see whether machine-intensive products or products with long setup times are being under- or over-costed. When changing the allocation basis, quantify the effect on product costs and explain why the new basis better reflects actual operations. Retain the approval and effective date.","applied":"Choose an allocation basis according to the relationship between the cost and the activity that causes it. Floor area, machine hours, labor hours and units produced may each be suitable in different circumstances; one basis will not accurately represent every overhead item. Separate fixed and variable costs when analyzing capacity, and state which expenses are included in overhead. Periodically review significant allocations and check whether changes in product mix are distorting the cost assigned to individual styles.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Manufacturing Overhead and Cost Allocation\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Manufacturing Overhead and Cost Allocation</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Manufacturing Overhead and Cost Allocation</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Build Cost Pools</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Select Allocation Bases</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Calculate and Reconcile</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Review Variances</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":13},"14":{"title":"Budget Variance Analysis","cat":"Finance and Factory Costs","overview":"Budget variance analysis compares actual results with the budget or forecast for the same scope and period. The total variance is only an initial signal; further analysis should separate the effects of price, quantity, product mix, labor hours, efficiency and timing.","method":"Lock the relevant budget version, obtain reconciled actual data, standardize units and prepare a variance bridge. For each material variance, identify an accountable owner, an evidence-based cause, the forecast impact and the required action.","caution":"Do not conclude that a department has exceeded its budget when actuals and budget use different scopes or classifications. A favorable variance is not necessarily positive if quality, safety or delivery performance has deteriorated.","deep":"For example, actual fabric cost may exceed budget because purchase prices increased, consumption per garment rose, defect rates worsened or the product mix changed. Price variance can be analyzed by comparing the actual purchase price with the standard price applied to actual quantity. Quantity variance can be analyzed by comparing actual consumption with the standard quantity allowed for actual output. The precise calculation must follow the company’s accounting system and policy. Each analysis line should identify the cost code, cost center, period, budget version, actual-data source and person responsible for confirmation. For labor costs, review working hours, overtime, SAM, good output and rework together. Causes may be classified as controllable or external, but responsibility must still be recorded under the factory’s process. A budget review should end with a decision: revise the forecast, retain the budget, investigate further or implement a corrective action. Recording a variance without defining the next step is not sufficient.","applied":"Break budget variances down into price, quantity, mix, timing and activity-level effects. Higher fabric costs, for example, may result from purchase prices, marker consumption, fabric-width changes or defects; each cause requires a different response. Reports should show the original budget, latest forecast, actual result, variance and person responsible for explanation. Avoid broad statements such as “market prices increased” unless supporting quotations or comparable data are available.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Budget Variance Analysis\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Budget Variance Analysis</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Budget Variance Analysis</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Lock the Budget Version</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Reconcile Actuals</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Break Down Variances</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Assign Owners and Actions</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":14},"15":{"title":"Factory Budgeting","cat":"Finance and Factory Costs","overview":"A factory budget is a time-based plan for resources, built from expected production volume, order mix, capacity, operating standards and pricing assumptions. It should be linked to production planning, manpower, materials, maintenance and cash flow.","method":"Prepare the budget by cost center and cost category. Document assumptions for volume, product mix, SAM, efficiency, unit prices, overtime, utilities, maintenance and capital expenditure. Review the assumptions with department heads, approve a baseline version and establish a controlled process for updating the forecast.","caution":"Do not prepare the budget by mechanically adding or subtracting a percentage from the previous year’s figures. A budget should not be treated as an unchangeable limit when business conditions shift; any revision must be controlled and explained.","deep":"Start with the monthly order plan, convert it into standard minutes and manpower requirements, and then estimate materials, utilities, maintenance and support costs. Separate volume assumptions from price assumptions so actual variances can be traced to quantity or rate changes. Prepare at least a base case and a scenario involving a product-mix change or material delay, and define the triggers for updating the forecast. A machine investment budget should include the operational objective, purchase price, installation, training, maintenance and capacity impact—not just the machine’s purchase price. During the year, update the forecast using the latest information while retaining the original budget as the comparison baseline. Record the preparer, reviewers, approver, version and revision history. At period-end, compare actual results with both budget and forecast, and document assumption errors to improve the next budgeting cycle.","applied":"Build the budget from verifiable operating assumptions: expected orders, working days, number of lines, capacity, workforce mix, wage levels, maintenance needs, utilities and investment plans. Separate recurring operating expenses from capital expenditure, and identify committed costs versus costs that can still be adjusted. After approval, update the forecast when orders or input prices change, while retaining the original budget for variance analysis. Any expenditure above an authorized limit must follow the approval route and be supported by a clear business reason.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Factory Budgeting\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Factory Budgeting</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Factory Budgeting</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Order Plan</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Cost Assumptions</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Budget Scenarios</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Monitor the Forecast</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":15},"16":{"title":"Cash Flow and Working Capital","cat":"Finance and Factory Costs","overview":"Cash flow records when money is actually received and paid, which is different from accounting profit. A factory may recognize revenue and still face a cash shortage because of inventory, receivables, material deposits or payment terms.","method":"Prepare a weekly or monthly cash-flow forecast showing opening balances, customer receipts, supplier payments, payroll, taxes, capital expenditure and borrowing. Update it using receivables aging and shipment and receipt schedules; distinguish confirmed amounts from estimates.","caution":"Do not use revenue figures as a substitute for cash receipts. Do not overlook payment terms, foreign currencies, credit limits or obligations falling due.","deep":"An order may require fabric to be purchased several weeks before shipment, while payment is received only after delivery and the customer has fulfilled the agreed payment conditions. The forecast should therefore link cash movements to material purchasing, production, shipment, documentation and expected collection milestones. Monitor DSO, receivables aging, inventory and payable terms as working-capital drivers; definitions and calculations must remain consistent within the company. Prepare a due-date schedule rather than relying only on monthly totals, so weeks with potential cash shortages are visible. For foreign-currency transactions, record the original currency, exchange rate used and exposure to rate movements. Model scenarios such as shipment delays, late customer payments or higher supplier deposits. Possible responses may include negotiating payment schedules, adjusting purchasing plans or using an already approved credit facility. Restrict access to the forecast and control who can change financial data.","applied":"A factory’s cash position is affected by when it buys materials, pays suppliers, manufactures goods, ships orders and collects from customers. Accounting profit does not mean that cash is available; growth in orders can increase working-capital pressure when materials must be purchased before customer payment is received. Prepare a weekly or monthly forecast showing major receipts and payments, due dates and scenarios involving shipment or collection delays. Reconcile the forecast with bank statements and actual receivables and payables, then update the projections.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Cash Flow and Working Capital\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Cash Flow and Working Capital</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Cash Flow and Working Capital</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Cash Collection Schedule</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Cash Payment Schedule</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Working Capital</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Shortfall Scenarios</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":16},"17":{"title":"Asset Management and Depreciation","cat":"Finance and Factory Costs","overview":"Asset management and depreciation connect accounting records with the actual condition and location of factory equipment. Each asset should have an identification code, location, responsible custodian, commissioning date, original cost, condition and maintenance history. Depreciation must follow the applicable accounting policy.","method":"Reconcile the fixed-asset register with physical inspections. Record movements, transfers, disposals and changes in asset condition. Track maintenance schedules and machine downtime to assess how effectively assets are being used.","caution":"Do not choose a useful life or depreciation method outside the applicable accounting policy and relevant requirements. Book value is not the same as market value or the equipment’s current production capability.","deep":"When a sewing machine is moved from Line A to Line B, update its location, responsible custodian and cost center if required by the factory’s procedure. If a machine remains out of service for an extended period, distinguish its continued presence in the asset register from its actual production capacity. Asset records should link the serial number, model, accessories, manual, maintenance schedule and repair history. When purchasing a new machine, account for installation, commissioning and training costs in accordance with the company’s accounting policy; do not decide independently whether these costs should be capitalized or expensed. Periodic physical counts should address missing assets, shared equipment and machines with major component replacements. Management reports may track asset age, utilization, downtime and maintenance cost, but these indicators do not replace accounting decisions about depreciation.","applied":"The asset register should include an identification code, location, user or custodian, commissioning date, original cost, condition, maintenance schedule and repair records. Physical verification must be reconciled with the accounting register, with movements, disposals and retired equipment clearly documented. Depreciation depends on the accounting policy, asset type and requirements in the relevant jurisdiction; one useful life should not be applied to every machine. Asset data should support repair, replacement, rental or transfer decisions based on total life-cycle cost.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Asset Management and Depreciation\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Asset Management and Depreciation</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Asset Management and Depreciation</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Asset Identification</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Physical Verification</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Depreciation Under Policy</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Track the Asset Life Cycle</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":17},"18":{"title":"Preventive Maintenance and Equipment Reliability","cat":"Finance and Factory Costs","overview":"Preventive maintenance consists of scheduled activities intended to reduce the likelihood of equipment failure and maintain operating condition. Reliability should be assessed through failure frequency, restoration time, operational impact and equipment history—not simply the number of maintenance calls.","method":"Create an equipment register and classify machines by criticality. Use manufacturer guidance to set cleaning, lubrication and inspection schedules, along with clear criteria for requesting maintenance. Record work orders, causes, spare parts, downtime and the results of post-repair testing.","caution":"Do not extend maintenance intervals merely to protect output when signs of failure may affect safety or product quality. Hazardous electrical or mechanical work must be performed by competent personnel under an energy-isolation procedure.","deep":"Classify equipment according to the consequences of failure: bottleneck machines, equipment with safety implications, shared machines or machines with difficult-to-source spare parts. For each category, define pre-shift checks, periodic maintenance and stop-work conditions. When the same machine repeatedly fails, replacing the component is not enough; investigate root causes such as alignment, spare-part quality, operating practices, dust conditions or inadequate lubrication. Track MTBF and MTTR only when operating time and repair time are recorded consistently. Do not compare these measures across machine groups with different operating conditions without explaining the differences. After a repair, run the machine and confirm seam quality before returning it to production. Review downtime alongside lost output and the production schedule when prioritizing maintenance resources.","applied":"Base the maintenance plan on manufacturer recommendations, equipment criticality, failure history and operating conditions. Distinguish daily operator checks from technical maintenance that requires specialist skills. Record machine hours, failure type, spare parts, downtime, cause and post-repair test results. Recurring failures require root-cause analysis rather than repeated parts replacement. Reliability indicators are meaningful only when operating time and downtime are defined consistently.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Preventive Maintenance and Equipment Reliability\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Preventive Maintenance and Equipment Reliability</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Preventive Maintenance and Equipment Reliability</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Classify Equipment Criticality</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Schedule Preventive Maintenance</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Record Work Orders</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Verify After Repair</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":18},"19":{"title":"Energy and Utilities Management","cat":"Assets, Safety and Environment","overview":"Energy and utilities management covers electricity, compressed air, steam, water, ventilation and other systems that support production. Consumption must be interpreted in the context of output, operating hours, equipment type and factory conditions.","method":"Map meters and measurement points. Record readings at defined intervals and allocate consumption by area or equipment where a sound basis exists. Check for compressed-air leaks, idle running, lighting issues and operating schedules; confirm safety requirements before any intervention.","caution":"Do not compare total kWh between two months without considering working days, production volume and equipment changes. Do not make unauthorized interventions in electrical, steam or pressurized systems.","deep":"Energy use per garment can be useful, but it may be misleading when styles require different machine time or when a month contains more non-production days. Depending on data quality, monitor total consumption alongside kWh per machine hour or kWh per standard minute. When consumption rises, check meter accuracy, shifts, idle machines, air leaks, new equipment and production-schedule changes before attributing the increase to operators. An energy-saving initiative needs a baseline, measurement boundary, trial period, production conditions and confirmation that quality and safety have not been compromised. Keep inspection records, leak locations, corrective actions and post-repair readings. Equipment adjustments must be made only by authorized personnel in accordance with technical procedures.","applied":"Monitor electricity, compressed air, steam, water and other utilities by area or critical equipment to identify abnormal consumption. When using consumption per garment, use good-quality output as the denominator and define which products or processes are included. Check compressed-air leaks, idle-running time, equipment start/stop schedules and steam-system performance in accordance with technical guidance. Before reporting savings, compare results with a baseline adjusted for operating hours, output and, where relevant, weather conditions.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Energy and Utilities Management\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Energy and Utilities Management</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Energy and Utilities Management</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Utilities Map</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Measure and Normalize</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Identify Losses</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Verify Savings</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":19},"20":{"title":"Occupational Safety and Risk Assessment","cat":"Assets, Safety and Environment","overview":"Occupational safety must be managed according to the hazards associated with each task and item of equipment. A risk assessment is not a sign-off form; it must identify who may be affected, the exposure scenario, the potential severity and the control measures required.","method":"Identify hazards around machines, cutting areas, warehouses, chemical storage, electrical systems and walkways. Apply the hierarchy of controls: elimination or substitution, engineering controls, administrative controls and then personal protective equipment (PPE). Verify that the controls remain effective after implementation.","caution":"Do not use PPE as a substitute for feasible engineering controls. Procedures, training and legal limits must reflect the jurisdiction and the factory’s actual activities.","deep":"When assessing a sewing machine, consider needle punctures, moving parts, electricity, noise, posture and cleaning or maintenance tasks. In cutting areas, assess knives, stacked materials, roll handling and lifting equipment. Record each hazard, the people who may be exposed, existing controls, residual risk and the person responsible. After installing a guard or changing the layout, observe actual work to ensure employees do not need to remove the guard or create another unsafe route. Incidents and near misses should be reported, investigated for systemic causes and used to share relevant lessons. Periodic inspections must confirm that controls are actually being used, not merely documented. If a serious hazard is identified, stop the work in accordance with the authorized procedure and response plan.","applied":"Assess risks from actual work activities, including cleaning, needle replacement, jam clearing, material handling and maintenance. For each hazard, record who may be affected, the potential severity, likelihood, existing controls and any additional action required. Prioritize eliminating or reducing hazards at source before relying on PPE. Employees need guidance on reporting near misses and a feedback process. Review the risk assessment whenever machines, layouts or working methods change.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Occupational Safety and Risk Assessment\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Occupational Safety and Risk Assessment</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Occupational Safety and Risk Assessment</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Identify Hazards</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Assess Risks</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Select Control Measures</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Verify Effectiveness</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":20},"21":{"title":"Fire Safety and Emergency Response","cat":"Assets, Safety and Environment","overview":"Fire safety and emergency response must be based on the facility’s ignition sources, combustible load, building layout, escape routes and response capability. Fabric, cartons, fiber dust, finishing chemicals and electrical systems present different types of risk.","method":"Inspect escape routes, safety signs, firefighting equipment, alarm systems, electrical sources and storage areas. Assign responsible personnel and define alarm activation, evacuation, assembly-point and headcount procedures. Conduct drills according to the site plan and applicable local requirements.","caution":"Never obstruct exits with goods, pallets or trolleys. Use firefighting equipment only when trained and when conditions permit; evacuate first whenever the situation is dangerous.","deep":"The emergency plan should show the actual routes from cutting, sewing lines, the fabric warehouse, chemical storage and office areas to the assembly points. Check that exit doors can be opened, routes are not blocked by pallets, and emergency lighting and signs remain visible during a power failure. Maintain information about people who may need evacuation assistance under appropriate access controls. After each drill, record the evacuation time, congestion points, people not yet accounted for and communication failures. Assign an owner and due date to every corrective action. Inspection of extinguishers, fire detection systems and legal compliance must be carried out by authorized personnel in accordance with current requirements. Do not change the extinguishing agent or equipment location without an appropriate assessment.","applied":"An emergency plan should reflect the site layout, stored materials, potential ignition sources, escape routes, firefighting equipment and the response team’s capabilities. Check whether fabric rolls, cartons or trolleys obstruct exits. Directional signs and assembly points must remain identifiable during a power failure or in smoky conditions. Drills should capture evacuation time, bottlenecks, unclear responsibilities and corrective actions. Legal requirements and inspection frequency depend on the facility’s location and the competent authorities.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Fire Safety and Emergency Response\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Fire Safety and Emergency Response</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Fire Safety and Emergency Response</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Identify Fire Hazards</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Plan Evacuation Routes</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Assign Response Roles</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Drill and Correct</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":21},"22":{"title":"Ergonomics and Occupational Health","cat":"Assets, Safety and Environment","overview":"Ergonomics examines how people, tasks, tools and the working environment fit together. In garment manufacturing, prolonged static postures, repetitive movements, hand force, visual demands, table height and material presentation can affect workers’ health and the consistency of their operations.","method":"Observe actual work performed by different operators and at different times. Assess the neck, shoulders, back, wrists, legs, reach distances and lighting. Trial adjustments to chairs, tables, pedals, material racks and workstation layout. Gather worker feedback and monitor the results after changes are introduced.","caution":"Do not treat musculoskeletal discomfort as solely an individual problem or expect workers to adapt to poorly designed workstations. Never remove or modify machine guards to increase operating speed.","deep":"Assess a sewing workstation by first considering a neutral working posture: the back is supported, the shoulders do not remain raised, the neck is not held in prolonged flexion, the wrists are not forced into awkward angles, and the pedal is within comfortable foot reach. Do not impose one chair or table dimension on every operator; adjustments must account for the individual, machine and product. Observe the work cycle, reach distance to material bins, frequency of trunk rotation and force required to guide fabric. Trial moving materials closer to the work area, adjusting seat height or adding a support. Then confirm that visibility, stitch quality and pedal access remain suitable. Record feedback, early symptoms and exposure duration. Reassess after a layout change or production-rate increase. Ergonomic controls must be maintained in daily operations, not just noted during an inspection.","applied":"Occupational-health assessment should consider repetitive workload, static posture, manual handling, noise, dust, heat, lighting and exposure duration. Analyze work groups and shifts to identify cumulative risks; do not draw conclusions from a single case. Controls should prioritize workstation and seating design, working height, assistive tools, ventilation and suitable job rotation. Health records must be kept confidential and used only by authorized personnel in accordance with applicable requirements.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Ergonomics and Occupational Health\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Ergonomics and Occupational Health</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Ergonomics and Occupational Health</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Observe Posture</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Assess Reach Zones</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Adjust the Workstation</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Reassess</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":22},"23":{"title":"Labor Compliance and Personnel Records","cat":"Assets, Safety and Environment","overview":"Labor compliance and personnel records must accurately reflect employment relationships, working hours, wages, rest periods, training, safety and reporting obligations applicable in the facility’s jurisdiction. Each record needs a defined source, retention period and access permission.","method":"Create a register of applicable obligations, responsible owners, required forms and supporting evidence. Reconcile employment contracts, personnel files, time records, payroll, overtime, leave, training and safety records. Check that the current versions of policies and forms are in use.","caution":"Do not adopt contract templates or internal rules from another country without legal review for the jurisdiction in which the factory operates. Do not collect personal information beyond legitimate and lawful business needs.","deep":"A personnel-file review should sample different employee groups, such as new hires, probationary employees, night-shift workers where applicable, employees who work overtime, those on extended leave and former employees. Check consistency across contracts, transfer decisions, shift schedules, attendance records and payslips. For each discrepancy, record its nature, the employees and periods affected, and the corrective action. Never alter an original record without retaining an audit trail. Schedule periodic reviews when legislation, company policies or customer requirements change. Conclusions about working hours, wage levels, insurance and record retention must be confirmed by qualified HR or legal personnel against local requirements. Training material provides a control framework; it does not replace legal advice.","applied":"Personnel records should have a document inventory, assigned custodian, retention period and access rules. Check the completeness of contracts, working-time information, wage records, training, safety documentation and other records required by the law where the factory operates. For temporary labor and contractors, clarify the responsibilities of both the factory and the labor provider. Do not copy a sample personnel-file system from another market without reviewing local legal requirements.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Labor Compliance and Personnel Records\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Labor Compliance and Personnel Records</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Labor Compliance and Personnel Records</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">List Applicable Duties</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Review Personnel Records</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Address Gaps</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Review Changes</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":23},"24":{"title":"Worker Dialogue, Grievances and Industrial Relations","cat":"Assets, Safety and Environment","overview":"Worker dialogue and industrial relations provide mechanisms for receiving, addressing and responding to issues between employees and the business. A credible system needs accessible reporting channels, appropriate confidentiality, response deadlines and safeguards against retaliation.","method":"Publish the available reporting channels, receiving contacts, severity-classification method, acknowledgement deadlines, investigators and escalation levels. Record cases on a need-to-know basis, protect personal information and communicate outcomes as appropriate.","caution":"Do not promise absolute confidentiality when an investigation or reporting obligation may require information to be shared. A manager who is the subject of a complaint must not control the entire case or decide its outcome alone.","deep":"Distinguish concerns involving working conditions, wages, harassment, safety, shift allocation and interpersonal conflict because each may require a different response. On receipt of a grievance, acknowledge it, assess any immediate risk and arrange interim protection where necessary. Investigate using evidence, interview relevant parties fairly and keep controlled records. Investigators should disclose conflicts of interest and escalate a case when they cannot act independently. Once a finding is reached, communicate the portion of the outcome that can appropriately be shared, monitor corrective actions and check for retaliation. Trend reports should summarize issue types and affected areas without unnecessarily identifying individuals. Regular dialogue is effective when suggestions have an assigned owner and response date, rather than being recorded only in meeting minutes.","applied":"A grievance mechanism should allow workers to raise concerns about safety, pay, working hours, management or discrimination without fear of retaliation. The procedure must specify reporting channels, response times, case owners, confidentiality principles and escalation routes when an issue remains unresolved. Track trends by issue type and department while avoiding disclosure of complainants’ identities. Dialogue is meaningful when the company responds with actions and communicates appropriate outcomes, not merely when meetings are held.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Worker Dialogue, Grievances and Industrial Relations\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Worker Dialogue, Grievances and Industrial Relations</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Worker Dialogue, Grievances and Industrial Relations</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Receive the Grievance</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Classify and Protect</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Investigate Independently</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Respond and Follow Up</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":24},"25":{"title":"Contractor and Outsourced-Service Management","cat":"Assets, Safety and Environment","overview":"Contractors and outsourced service providers may support security, cleaning, transportation, maintenance, waste treatment or subcontracted production. The factory must still control risks at its site and define responsibilities under contracts, applicable law and internal procedures.","method":"Before selection, assess the contractor’s capability, required permits, personnel, training, insurance, equipment and emergency arrangements. Before work begins, issue permits where required, communicate site hazards, supervise the work and formally accept the deliverables.","caution":"Do not assume that contractors carry all responsibility for safety or compliance. Do not authorize hazardous work without the required risk assessment and approval.","deep":"For an electrical-maintenance contractor, the work documentation should identify the scope, supervisor, authorized workers, energy-isolation arrangements, work zone and conditions for returning the machine to service. For waste services, verify the waste category, transfer documentation and receiving organization against applicable requirements. Evaluate contractors on quality, timeliness, incidents, compliance and service feedback—not price alone. When a contractor is replaced, recover access cards, accounts, keys and system permissions, and check for outstanding work or records. Contracts should define service levels, reporting duties, incident notification, insurance, confidentiality and the process for correcting nonconformities. Site owners should verify performance rather than relying solely on a contractor’s self-declaration.","applied":"Contractor control should begin before work is awarded and continue through mobilization, site access, execution and close-out. Verify competence and permits, communicate hazards, define supervision and emergency arrangements, and confirm that work is completed safely. For outsourced processes, establish acceptance criteria, traceability, change notification and access to relevant records. Review performance and incidents periodically, and close access rights when the service ends.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Contractor and Outsourced-Service Management\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Contractor and Outsourced-Service Management</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Contractor and Outsourced-Service Management</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Evaluate the Contractor</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Authorize the Work</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Supervise On-Site Work</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Accept and Review</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":25},"26":{"title":"Environmental Management System","cat":"Assets, Safety and Environment","overview":"An environmental management system provides a structured way to identify environmental aspects, determine applicable obligations, control operations and improve performance. In a garment factory, relevant aspects may include energy and water use, chemicals, air emissions, wastewater, solid waste and transportation.","method":"Define the system scope and responsibilities. Identify environmental aspects and impacts by process, determine applicable legal and other obligations, establish operational controls, set measurable objectives and monitor performance. Maintain evidence of inspections, training, incidents, monitoring and corrective actions.","caution":"Do not treat an environmental policy or certificate as proof that daily operations are controlled. Verify actual site conditions, current permits, monitoring results and the competence of service providers.","deep":"The aspect register should reflect actual activities, including fabric storage, cutting, sewing, printing, washing, finishing, boiler operation, chemical storage and wastewater treatment where present. Rate aspects using defined criteria such as severity, likelihood, scale, legal relevance and stakeholder concern; document the method so different departments apply it consistently. Operational controls should cover normal, abnormal and emergency conditions—for example, a chemical leak, oil spill or failure of a treatment system. Each monitoring indicator needs a clear data source and frequency, such as electricity, water, waste by category or required monitoring results. When a result exceeds an internal or legal limit, follow the response procedure and assess the impact. Verify waste-transport and treatment contractors through transfer records and their authorization under local requirements. Internal audits should include site inspections, equipment checks and operator interviews, not document review alone.","applied":"An environmental management system should identify significant aspects arising from actual operations, including energy, water and chemical consumption, air emissions, wastewater, waste and transportation. Each aspect needs operating controls, an accountable owner, monitoring records and a response plan for conditions outside acceptable limits. Environmental objectives require a defined baseline, calculation boundary and data source. Do not compare reporting periods without explaining changes in production volume or factory scope.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Environmental Management System\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Environmental Management System</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Environmental Management System</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Define the Scope</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Assess Environmental Aspects</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Control Operations</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Evaluate Compliance</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":26},"27":{"title":"Waste Management and the Circular Economy","cat":"Operations and Continuous Improvement","overview":"Waste management requires segregation at source, safe storage, quantity records and transfer to appropriate service providers in accordance with applicable requirements. Circular-economy practices in a factory can begin with waste prevention, efficient material use, suitable internal reuse and transfer to capable recycling or recovery partners.","method":"Map waste streams by process, define labels and storage areas, record weights at an established frequency, control transfer documents and evaluate contractors. Classify fabric scraps, cartons, plastics and chemicals separately according to their properties and applicable rules.","caution":"Do not reuse materials that may be contaminated or fail product requirements. Never mix hazardous waste with ordinary waste to reduce handling costs.","deep":"Fabric waste analysis should distinguish material generated by marker and cutting operations, end-of-roll remnants, material defects, development samples and rejected garments. If the factory weighs all fabric waste as one total, it will be difficult to identify the source of losses. Record quantities by style or area where the data allows, then compare them with incoming fabric or output to identify trends. For any reuse program, define its purpose, quality requirements, hygiene conditions and approval owner; unsuitable material must not be returned to products. Storage areas need segregation, labels, spill prevention and stock-duration controls as required. At transfer, reconcile the quantity, waste category, vehicle and supporting documents. Measure waste reduction against a comparable baseline and avoid reporting an improvement that is explained only by lower production volume.","applied":"Segregating waste where it is generated helps keep fabric scraps, paper, packaging, metal, chemicals and hazardous waste separate under local requirements. Record the quantity, source, collection company, transfer documentation and final destination. Before sending fabric remnants or surplus material for recycling, assess whether reuse is suitable; fiber composition, quality and finishing chemicals may limit available options. Do not describe a material stream as recycled without evidence of an authorized receiving organization and an actual processing route.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Waste Management and the Circular Economy\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Waste Management and the Circular Economy</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Waste Management and the Circular Economy</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Map Waste Streams</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Segregate at Source</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Weigh and Trace</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Prevent and Transfer</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":27},"28":{"title":"Data Governance and Information Security","cat":"Operations and Continuous Improvement","overview":"Data governance and information security ensure that operational data has clear definitions, ownership, access permissions and change history. Factory data may include technical packs, bills of materials, patterns, costing, productivity, personnel records, quality data and customer information, each with a different sensitivity level.","method":"Classify data, assign owners and authoritative sources, define read and edit permissions, and establish naming and storage rules. Control individual accounts, backups, file sharing, mobile devices and the process for removing access when employees leave or change roles.","caution":"Do not send technical packs, personal data or costing information through unauthorized channels. Avoid shared accounts that make it impossible to identify who changed a record.","deep":"A technical pack may be downloaded and edited outside the main system. Without a style code, revision, effective date and approval record, a production line may use an obsolete specification. Establish one authoritative source and require printed copies used on the shop floor to show the applicable revision. For dashboards, publish KPI definitions and data sources so departments do not calculate the same measure differently. Apply least-privilege access: permission to view does not automatically include permission to edit or export. When an employee changes roles or leaves, review access to applications, email, shared drives and devices. Backups should be restored in periodic tests; having a copy without testing recovery does not demonstrate recoverability. Record security incidents, including timing, affected data scope and response actions, in accordance with the security policy.","applied":"Data governance should define data owners, who may create, edit and approve records, the authoritative source and the retention period. For style, BOM, supplier, personnel and quality data, distinguish sensitive information from information that may be shared with business partners. Role-based access, appropriate authentication, backups and change logs help trace incorrect changes or data leakage. For shared spreadsheets, establish naming, version-control and editing rules instead of circulating multiple email attachments.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Data Governance and Information Security\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Data Governance and Information Security</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Data Governance and Information Security</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Classify Data</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Set Authoritative Sources</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Control Access</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Back Up and Respond</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":28},"29":{"title":"Factory-Level KPIs and Management Cadence","cat":"Operations and Continuous Improvement","overview":"Factory-level KPIs should connect business objectives with production, quality, delivery, cost, people and safety performance. Every KPI needs a definition, data source, reporting frequency, owner, alert threshold and a defined response when performance deviates.","method":"Build a KPI tree from factory objectives down to departments, while limiting the number of measures used in routine management. Check the relationship between leading and lagging indicators, then set update cycles by shift, day, week or month.","caution":"Do not use KPIs for rewards or penalties while definitions and data are unstable. Do not improve one measure at the expense of another important outcome.","deep":"Read accepted output together with first-pass yield (FPY), rework, work in progress (WIP) and delivery performance. If output rises while defects and work-in-progress also increase, the factory may simply be transferring problems to the next process. For each KPI, document the formula, unit, denominator, workforce scope and cut-off time. Manage revisions whenever a definition changes. Distinguish lagging measures, such as on-time delivery, from leading indicators, such as material readiness or completion of pre-production checks. Management meetings should focus on deviations beyond agreed thresholds, evidence-based causes, decisions required and accountable owners. Review the KPI set periodically to remove measures that no longer support decisions and to detect behavior that manipulates reported results.","applied":"A KPI should be linked to a decision that a manager can actually take. Each measure needs a formula, data source, owner, frequency, target and response when results fall outside the agreed range. Use a balanced set covering delivery, quality, productivity, cost, safety, people and the environment. Too many KPIs turn management meetings into number-reading sessions instead of problem-solving. Check consistency across production, QA, planning and finance dashboards before using the data to evaluate performance.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Factory-Level KPIs and Management Cadence\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Factory-Level KPIs and Management Cadence</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Factory-Level KPIs and Management Cadence</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Set Factory Objectives</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Define KPIs</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Set Thresholds and Owners</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Run the Review Cycle</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":29},"30":{"title":"Management Meetings and Escalation","cat":"Operations and Continuous Improvement","overview":"Management meetings turn operational data into decisions and actions. An effective meeting has a clear purpose, information prepared in advance, decision-makers in attendance and an action log that tracks follow-through.","method":"Structure the agenda around safety and quality, output and schedule, materials and equipment, workforce, order risks and decisions requiring escalation. Every action needs an owner, deadline, closure evidence and a defined escalation route if overdue.","caution":"Do not turn meetings into read-outs of reports or exercises in blaming individuals. An action is not closed merely because an email has been sent; its outcome must be verified.","deep":"A material-shortage issue should be presented with the material code, shortage quantity, affected orders, required date, verified stock, supplier ETA and any permitted alternative. The meeting chair must make the decision or assign it to someone with the authority to act; the issue must not remain indefinitely in the minutes. Use one action log with clear statuses such as open, in progress, awaiting decision and closed after verification. Escalation thresholds should reflect impact and elapsed time. For example, a risk of line stoppage or shipment delay must be raised immediately under the procedure rather than waiting for the weekly meeting. At the end of the meeting, restate each decision, owner and due date. At the next meeting, review closure evidence. If an issue recurs, move beyond case-by-case fixes and investigate the underlying system cause.","applied":"Management meetings should distinguish issues requiring an immediate decision from those that need deeper analysis outside the meeting. Each agenda item should include the data, impact, available options, proposer and required decision date. Escalate when an issue exceeds the owner’s authority, threatens safety, affects delivery or remains unresolved beyond the agreed response time. Minutes must record the decision, accountable owner, review date and closure status—not merely summarize the discussion.","diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Management Meetings and Escalation\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Management Meetings and Escalation</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Management Meetings and Escalation</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Prepare Meeting Data</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Prioritize Issues</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Decide and Escalate</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Track Actions</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>","id":30},"31":{"title":"Continuous Improvement and Kaizen","cat":"Điều hành và cải tiến","overview":"Continuous improvement is a disciplined approach to identifying and removing causes of waste, defects, delays and unnecessary effort. Kaizen emphasizes frequent, practical improvements led by the people who understand the work, supported by management and measured against clear operating results.","method":"Establish a baseline for the process, define the problem in observable terms and collect evidence before proposing a solution. Involve operators and relevant support functions, test changes on a controlled scale, compare the results with the baseline and standardize improvements that prove effective. Assign an owner and review date.","caution":"Do not treat the number of suggestions or Kaizen events as proof of improvement. A change must be evaluated for quality, safety, workload and downstream effects before it becomes the new standard.","deep":"A useful improvement starts with a specific gap, such as repeated rework at a particular sewing operation or excessive movement between material racks. Observe the work as it is actually performed and distinguish symptoms from causes. Use process mapping, check sheets, Pareto analysis or the 5 Whys where appropriate, but verify each proposed cause with evidence. A small trial might reposition a component bin, revise the work sequence or clarify an inspection point. Compare output, defect rate, cycle time, ergonomic impact and work-in-progress before and after the trial under comparable conditions. If the result is positive, update the relevant SOP, visual instruction, training and line layout. If the trial does not work, record what was learned rather than forcing the change. Supervisors should review whether the improvement is sustained after the initial attention has passed.","applied":"Select one recurring problem from a production line and document its location, frequency, impact and current method. Ask operators what makes the task difficult, then observe the operation and validate the likely causes. Trial one change at a time where practical. Confirm that the change improves the intended measure without creating quality, safety or workload problems elsewhere. Update the standard and check the result again after implementation.","id":31,"diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Continuous Improvement and Kaizen\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Continuous Improvement and Kaizen</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Continuous Improvement and Kaizen</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Baseline</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Improvement Hypothesis</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Controlled Trial</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Standardize and Monitor</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"32":{"title":"Change Management and Capital Projects","cat":"Điều hành và cải tiến","overview":"Change management controls modifications to products, processes, equipment, systems, organization and working methods. Capital projects require additional control over business justification, technical specifications, investment approval, implementation, commissioning and expected benefits.","method":"Define the reason for change, scope, affected processes, stakeholders, risks, budget, schedule and approval authority. Assess impacts on quality, capacity, safety, compliance, training, data and customer commitments. Use a controlled implementation plan with acceptance criteria, rollback arrangements where feasible and post-implementation review.","caution":"Do not introduce a process or equipment change solely because installation is complete. Production release requires verification that safety, quality, capability, documentation and trained personnel are ready.","deep":"For a new automatic spreading or cutting system, the project case should identify the current constraint, expected capacity improvement, material utilization assumptions, labor impact, maintenance needs and total cost of ownership. The technical specification should define product range, material limits, accuracy, interfaces, utilities, guarding and required documentation. Before purchase, compare supplier proposals against the same criteria and verify references where appropriate. During installation, control site access, electrical and mechanical work, and any production disruption. Commissioning should test representative materials and styles, not just an empty machine cycle. Record acceptance results, unresolved defects, operator and maintenance training, spare parts and warranty conditions. After the agreed stabilization period, compare actual throughput, quality, downtime and cost with the approved business case. Changes to scope, cost or expected benefits must be reviewed by the designated authority.","applied":"Use a change request for any modification that may affect product conformity, production capacity, worker safety, delivery, cost or customer approval. Record the reason, affected styles and processes, risk assessment, required tests, approvers, effective date and communication plan. For capital investment, track approved budget, commitments, actual expenditure, commissioning evidence and post-investment benefits. Close the project only when operational ownership and documentation have been transferred.","id":32,"diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Change Management and Capital Projects\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Change Management and Capital Projects</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Change Management and Capital Projects</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Proposed Change</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Risk and Cost Assessment</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Trial and Training</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Verify Benefits</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"33":{"title":"Crisis Management and Business Continuity","cat":"Điều hành và cải tiến","overview":"Crisis management addresses events that threaten people, operations, assets, information or the factory’s ability to meet commitments. Business continuity planning identifies critical activities, acceptable interruption limits, recovery priorities and the resources needed to resume operations.","method":"Identify credible disruption scenarios, assess their impact and rank critical processes. Define incident leadership, communication channels, emergency contacts, alternate resources, recovery steps and decision authority. Test the plan through exercises and update it when the facility, suppliers or operating model changes.","caution":"Do not assume that a written plan will work without tested contacts, available resources and clear authority. Life safety takes priority over production recovery or shipment targets.","deep":"A continuity plan for a garment factory may need to address fire, flooding, extended power loss, cyber incidents, critical machine failure, labor disruption, supplier interruption or loss of access to the site. For each scenario, identify which orders, processes, records and customer commitments are affected. Establish recovery priorities using actual dependencies: for example, production cannot resume merely because sewing machines are available if approved fabric, patterns, technical packs or quality personnel are inaccessible. Define alternate suppliers or sites only after checking capacity, quality approval, legal constraints, confidentiality and logistics. Contact lists must be current and accessible when the normal network is unavailable. Exercises should test realistic decisions, such as who authorizes order reallocation or customer notification. After an incident or exercise, record gaps, assign corrective actions and verify closure. Recovery time objectives should be based on business impact and realistic capability, not arbitrary promises.","applied":"Prepare a short scenario for a disruption that could stop production for one or more shifts. Identify the first actions, decision-maker, people to contact, affected orders, critical materials and recovery options. Check whether the plan can be accessed if the factory network or office is unavailable. Conduct a tabletop exercise and revise the plan based on delays, unclear responsibilities and missing resources.","id":33,"diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Crisis Management and Business Continuity\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Crisis Management and Business Continuity</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Crisis Management and Business Continuity</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Disruption Scenario</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Prioritize Critical Processes</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Continuity Options</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Exercise and Recovery</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"34":{"title":"Management Reporting for Executive Leadership","cat":"Điều hành và cải tiến","overview":"Executive management reporting condenses operational information into a reliable view of business performance, material risks and decisions required. Reports should explain what changed, why it matters, what management needs to decide and who is responsible for follow-up.","method":"Agree on a reporting calendar, standard KPI definitions, data owners and approval steps. Present results against target and prior periods, explain material variances, identify risks to orders and cash flow, and distinguish confirmed facts from estimates or forecasts.","caution":"Do not overload executive reports with raw operational detail or present unverified figures as final. Every significant number should have a traceable source, reporting period and definition.","deep":"A monthly factory report may cover shipment performance, production output, efficiency, first-pass yield, rework, material readiness, overtime, absenteeism, safety incidents, inventory, cost and cash exposure. The report should highlight exceptions rather than repeat every departmental figure. For example, a delivery risk should connect the affected order, remaining quantity, material status, available capacity, recovery option and decision deadline. Explain whether a variance is caused by volume, product mix, efficiency, price, scrap or timing. Use consistent cut-off dates and reconcile figures with the source systems before release. Where data is incomplete, state the limitation and the expected confirmation date. Executive decisions and action owners should be recorded so the next report can show whether the issue was resolved.","applied":"Prepare an executive summary that fits the management decision cycle. Start with the overall status, then show a small number of material variances, risks and decisions required. For each issue, state the impact, evidence, proposed response, accountable owner and latest decision date. Keep detailed calculations and supporting tables available as appendices rather than crowding the main report.","id":34,"diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Management Reporting for Executive Leadership\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Management Reporting for Executive Leadership</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Management Reporting for Executive Leadership</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Results vs. Targets</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Variances and Causes</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Emerging Risks</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Decisions Required</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"35":{"title":"Operational Maturity Roadmap","cat":"Điều hành và cải tiến","overview":"An operational maturity roadmap describes how a factory can progress from inconsistent, person-dependent practices toward controlled, measurable and continually improving processes. It provides a sequence of capability improvements rather than a list of software or equipment purchases.","method":"Assess current practices across leadership, process control, people capability, quality, planning, maintenance, data and improvement. Define evidence-based maturity levels, identify the most important gaps and prioritize initiatives according to business impact, readiness, cost and dependency.","caution":"Do not assign maturity levels based on policy documents or management opinion alone. Confirm that procedures are used consistently on the shop floor and that records reflect actual performance.","deep":"A factory may have a documented SOP but still depend on individual supervisors to explain critical steps. In that case, documentation exists but process deployment is not yet mature. Similarly, a dashboard does not demonstrate data-driven management if departments use different definitions or do not act on deviations. Assess each capability through documents, direct observation, interviews and operating records. A practical roadmap might first stabilize standard work and basic quality controls, then strengthen planning accuracy, maintenance discipline and skills development, followed by integrated data and predictive analysis where the underlying data is reliable. Each initiative should have a baseline, target, owner, resources, milestone and evidence of adoption. Review the roadmap periodically as customer requirements, product mix, technology and business priorities change.","applied":"Choose a small number of capabilities that constrain factory performance. Describe the current condition using evidence, define the desired operating practice and identify the gap in skills, process, technology or governance. Sequence initiatives so that foundational controls are established before advanced systems are introduced. Measure adoption and operating outcomes, not only project completion.","id":35,"diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Operational Maturity Roadmap\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Operational Maturity Roadmap</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Operational Maturity Roadmap</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Assess Current Capabilities</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Identify Capability Gaps</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Prioritize Initiatives</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Measure Maturity</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"36":{"title":"Integrated Exercise: Designing RACI, SOPs and the Factory Management Cadence","cat":"Thực hành tổng hợp","overview":"This integrated exercise combines responsibility design, standard operating procedures and management routines into one operating-control framework. The objective is to ensure that important factory decisions have clear ownership, work is performed consistently and performance issues are reviewed at the right level and frequency.","method":"Select a cross-functional process, such as production-order release, handling a quality hold or approving a material change. Map the process from trigger to final output. Define each decision and handoff, assign RACI roles, draft or revise the relevant SOP, and specify the records and escalation points. Then design the daily, weekly and monthly review cadence that checks whether the process is working.","caution":"Do not assign multiple Accountable owners to the same decision without a clear governance reason. An SOP must describe the actual approved method, and a meeting cadence must not become a substitute for operational controls or required approvals.","practice":"**Exercise scope**\n\nUse a factory process that crosses at least three functions. A suitable example is the release of a production order before bulk production begins. The process may involve merchandising, planning, warehouse, technical, quality and production teams.\n\n**Step 1 – Define the process boundary**\n\nState the trigger, required inputs, final output and conditions under which the process must stop. List the documents and data needed, such as the approved order details, technical pack, bill of materials, material status, capacity plan and quality requirements. Distinguish mandatory release conditions from information that can be completed later.\n\n**Step 2 – Build the responsibility matrix**\n\nList each decision or deliverable as a separate row. Assign Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed roles. Use job roles rather than individual names where possible. Confirm that each decision has an authorized owner and that the people assigned to perform the work have the information and resources required. Resolve gaps where two departments assume the other one is responsible.\n\n**Step 3 – Draft the SOP**\n\nWrite the sequence of activities in observable terms. For each step, identify the responsible role, input, action, acceptance criteria, record and next handoff. Include exception handling for missing materials, incomplete technical information, failed quality checks and changes after approval. Define document version, effective date and approval authority.\n\n**Step 4 – Establish the management cadence**\n\nDecide which indicators need shift-level, daily, weekly or monthly review. A daily meeting may address order readiness and immediate constraints; a weekly review may address capacity, supplier risks and recurring quality issues; a monthly review may examine trends, costs and improvement priorities. Each meeting must have a defined purpose and decision authority.\n\n**Step 5 – Test the framework**\n\nWalk through a real or simulated case. Check whether the assigned roles know what to do, whether the SOP provides enough information and whether an exception reaches the right decision-maker in time. Record unclear handoffs and revise the matrix or procedure before approval.\n\n**Required outputs**\n\n1. Process map showing the trigger, activities, decisions and handoffs.\n2. RACI matrix covering all critical decisions and deliverables.\n3. Controlled SOP with inputs, steps, acceptance criteria, records and exception handling.\n4. Daily, weekly and monthly management cadence with owners and indicators.\n5. A short test report listing gaps, corrective actions and approval status.","deep":"The value of this exercise lies in the connection between governance documents and daily execution. A RACI matrix that is not reflected in the SOP will leave operators and supervisors uncertain about who acts at each step. An SOP that is not reviewed through an appropriate management cadence may become outdated or fail to address recurring problems. Test the framework against exceptions, not only the normal sequence. For example, simulate a production order with an unapproved material substitution or a missing test report. Confirm who can place the order on hold, who evaluates the impact, who communicates with the customer and who authorizes release. The final design should make the decision path visible and preserve evidence of approvals and changes.","applied":"Apply the framework to a process that currently experiences delays, repeated clarification or unclear ownership. Interview the functions involved, compare their understanding of responsibilities and test the proposed SOP with the people who will use it. After approval, review actual cases during the first operating cycle and revise the controls where evidence shows that handoffs or escalation are still unclear.","id":36,"diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 800 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Integrated Exercise: Designing RACI, SOPs and the Factory Management Cadence\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Integrated Exercise: Designing RACI, SOPs and the Factory Management Cadence</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Integrated Exercise: Designing RACI, SOPs and the Factory Management Cadence</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Process Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Select a Sample Process</text><path d=\"190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Build the RACI and SOP</text><path d=\"388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Design the Review Cadence</text><path d=\"586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Test an Exception Scenario</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile evidence, accountable ownership and the applicable document revision.</text></svg></figure>"},"37":{"title":"Integrated Exercise: Workforce Planning, Skills and Labor Cost","cat":"Thực hành tổng hợp","overview":"This exercise connects production demand with labor capacity, skill availability and labor cost. The purpose is to develop a workforce plan that is feasible by operation and shift, rather than relying only on a total headcount estimate.","method":"Start with the confirmed order quantity, approved SAM, available working time and planned efficiency. Convert the workload into labor minutes, distribute the minutes by operation, compare required skills with the skill matrix, and identify gaps in staffing or training. Estimate labor cost using the applicable wage and allowance rules, then test alternative scenarios.","caution":"Do not treat a theoretical headcount calculation as proof that the line can meet the plan. Validate bottleneck operations, skill levels, attendance assumptions, machine availability and the wage rules applicable to the site.","practice":"**Exercise scope**\n\nPrepare a workforce and labor-cost plan for one garment style or a defined production order. Use approved source data and clearly label assumptions where actual figures are unavailable.\n\n**Step 1 – Confirm the workload**\n\nRecord the order quantity, required completion period, approved SAM and planned production calendar. Calculate total standard minutes as order quantity multiplied by SAM. Confirm that the SAM revision applies to the selected style and operation method.\n\n**Step 2 – Calculate available labor capacity**\n\nDetermine the usable minutes per worker for the planning period. State how breaks, planned absence, training time and other allowances are treated so that they are not counted twice. Apply a realistic planned efficiency and calculate the indicative direct-labor requirement. Keep indirect roles separate unless the costing method explicitly includes them.\n\n**Step 3 – Distribute workload by operation**\n\nBreak the total SAM into operation-level minutes using the approved operation breakdown. Identify bottlenecks, special-machine requirements and operations that need specific qualifications. Compare the required capacity with the available machines and trained operators.\n\n**Step 4 – Review the skills matrix**\n\nMap each required operation to available workers and their validated skill levels. Identify operations with only one qualified operator, workers requiring supervision and training needs. Propose cross-training or staffing adjustments and estimate the time needed before the skill can be relied upon independently.\n\n**Step 5 – Estimate labor cost**\n\nApply the relevant wage rates, piece-rate rules, allowances, overtime assumptions and employer costs according to the factory’s approved costing method and local requirements. State the calculation basis and distinguish direct labor from indirect labor. Do not assume that all workers have the same rate or productivity.\n\n**Step 6 – Test scenarios**\n\nCompare at least a base plan and a constrained scenario, such as lower efficiency, higher absenteeism or a shortage of qualified operators. Show the effect on headcount, overtime, output timing and labor cost. Identify the assumptions that have the greatest impact.\n\n**Required outputs**\n\n1. Workload calculation based on order quantity and approved SAM.\n2. Headcount estimate with clearly stated available-time and efficiency assumptions.\n3. Operation-level capacity and bottleneck review.\n4. Skills-gap and training plan linked to the skill matrix.\n5. Labor-cost estimate with the calculation basis and included cost elements.\n6. Scenario comparison and recommended staffing actions.","deep":"The calculations should be traceable from order quantity to operation-level workload and then to staffing and cost. A total headcount figure can conceal a shortage at a critical operation, so the plan must be checked against the operation breakdown and the skill matrix. Efficiency assumptions should reflect the style, learning curve, line condition and production history. Labor-cost estimates must use the correct wage basis and must not mix paid time, productive time and standard minutes without explaining the conversion. Scenario analysis helps management see whether the risk is best addressed through recruitment, training, line balancing, overtime or schedule changes.","applied":"Use this exercise before committing a line to a new order or style. Validate the SAM, available time, skills and machine requirements with the relevant functions. Review the plan after the first production days using actual output, efficiency, absenteeism and bottleneck data. Update the assumptions when the operating evidence differs materially from the original plan.","id":37,"diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Integrated Exercise: Workforce Planning, Skills and Labor Cost\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Integrated Exercise: Workforce Planning, Skills and Labor Cost</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Integrated Exercise: Workforce Planning, Skills and Labor Cost</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Implementation Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Output</text><path d=\"M190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Workforce</text><path d=\"M388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Cost</text><path d=\"M586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Skills Plan</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record the owner, data, deadline and evidence in line with the factory procedure.</text></svg></figure>"},"38":{"title":"Integrated Exercise: Factory Budget, Costing and Cash Flow","cat":"Thực hành tổng hợp","overview":"This exercise brings together factory budgeting, product and conversion costing, working-capital requirements and cash-flow planning. It helps managers understand how production volume, material consumption, labor, overhead, inventory and customer payment terms affect financial performance and liquidity.","method":"Define the planning period and production assumptions. Build the budget from order volume, material requirements, labor capacity, overhead and investment needs. Calculate unit cost using a consistent allocation method, then prepare a cash-flow schedule based on purchasing, production, shipment, invoicing and collection timing. Test changes in volume, material price, efficiency and payment delays.","caution":"Do not confuse accounting profit with available cash. Do not allocate overhead or calculate unit cost without stating the cost pool, allocation base, period and treatment of abnormal losses.","practice":"**Exercise scope**\n\nPrepare a simplified operating budget and cash-flow forecast for a factory or a defined production program. Use actual approved figures where available; otherwise, label assumptions and keep them consistent throughout the model.\n\n**Step 1 – Establish planning assumptions**\n\nRecord planned orders, product mix, production volume, shipment dates, selling prices or conversion charges as applicable, material consumption, approved purchase prices, SAM, wage rates, planned efficiency and operating calendar. Identify the source and date of each key assumption.\n\n**Step 2 – Build the operating budget**\n\nEstimate direct materials, direct labor, factory overhead and relevant selling or administrative costs according to the scope of the exercise. Separate fixed and variable costs where the data supports the distinction. Include planned maintenance, utilities, training, quality activities and capital expenditure where relevant. Avoid counting the same cost in more than one category.\n\n**Step 3 – Calculate unit cost**\n\nDefine the cost object, such as one garment, one dozen or one production order. State which costs are included and the allocation basis for shared overhead. Calculate the unit cost and show the effect of material yield, rework, scrap and efficiency assumptions. Keep abnormal losses visible rather than hiding them in a general rate.\n\n**Step 4 – Prepare the cash-flow schedule**\n\nMap the timing of deposits, material payments, payroll, utilities, other operating expenses, shipment, invoicing and customer collection. Include opening cash and any financing assumptions. Distinguish a purchase commitment from the date cash is actually paid, and distinguish shipment from customer collection.\n\n**Step 5 – Identify working-capital needs**\n\nEstimate the cash tied up in raw materials, work in progress and finished goods, taking account of supplier terms and customer payment terms. Identify periods when cash outflows occur before collections. State the peak funding requirement and the assumptions that drive it.\n\n**Step 6 – Run sensitivity scenarios**\n\nTest changes such as a material-price increase, lower production efficiency, delayed shipment, higher rework or slower customer collection. Show the effect on unit cost, margin where applicable, cash balance and peak working-capital requirement.\n\n**Required outputs**\n\n1. Assumption register with sources and dates.\n2. Operating budget by cost category.\n3. Unit-cost calculation with allocation basis.\n4. Period-based cash-flow forecast.\n5. Working-capital estimate and peak funding requirement.\n6. Sensitivity analysis and proposed management actions.","deep":"A factory can report an acceptable margin while facing a cash shortage because materials and payroll are paid before the customer settles the invoice. The model must therefore keep cost recognition and cash timing separate. Unit costing should use a consistent cost object and allocation basis, while the cash forecast should follow actual payment and collection dates. Test whether the factory can finance the production cycle under realistic delays. If the forecast depends on an unconfirmed deposit, supplier extension or customer payment date, show that dependency explicitly rather than treating it as guaranteed.","applied":"Use the model during order review, annual budgeting or a major change in material prices and payment terms. Reconcile forecast assumptions with purchasing, production, finance and merchandising. Update the cash forecast as orders are released, materials are purchased, shipments are delayed or invoices are collected. Escalate a projected cash shortfall early enough for management to evaluate financing, purchasing or production options.","id":38,"diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Integrated Exercise: Factory Budget, Costing and Cash Flow\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Integrated Exercise: Factory Budget, Costing and Cash Flow</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Integrated Exercise: Factory Budget, Costing and Cash Flow</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Implementation Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Budget</text><path d=\"M190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Actual</text><path d=\"M388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Forecast</text><path d=\"M586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Cash Flow</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record the owner, data, deadline and evidence in line with the factory procedure.</text></svg></figure>"},"39":{"title":"Integrated Exercise: Managing Factory Safety, Environment and Compliance","cat":"Thực hành tổng hợp","overview":"This exercise integrates workplace safety, environmental controls and compliance obligations into a practical factory-management review. It focuses on identifying risks in actual work areas, assigning accountable owners, maintaining evidence and ensuring that corrective actions are completed.","method":"Select representative factory areas and processes. Identify hazards, environmental aspects and applicable obligations; assess risks using the site’s approved methods; inspect existing controls; and prepare an action plan with owners, deadlines and verification evidence. Include routine, non-routine and emergency conditions.","caution":"Do not treat a completed checklist as proof of compliance. Legal obligations, permits, exposure limits and inspection requirements must be confirmed for the factory’s jurisdiction by competent personnel.","practice":"**Exercise scope**\n\nConduct a structured review of selected factory areas, such as the cutting room, sewing floor, chemical store, boiler or utility area, warehouse and waste-storage area. Adapt the scope to the processes actually present at the site.\n\n**Step 1 – Identify hazards and environmental aspects**\n\nObserve work activities, equipment, material storage, chemical handling, waste movement, energy use and emergency access. Record potential injury or health hazards and environmental aspects such as spills, emissions, wastewater, noise, dust and waste generation. Include abnormal and emergency situations.\n\n**Step 2 – Identify applicable obligations**\n\nList the permits, legal requirements, customer requirements and internal standards relevant to each area. Record the source, responsible owner, evidence required and review date. Refer legal interpretation to qualified personnel and competent authorities where necessary.\n\n**Step 3 – Assess risks and existing controls**\n\nApply the factory’s approved risk-assessment method. Consider severity, likelihood, exposure and the effectiveness of existing controls. Check whether controls are present, suitable, maintained and actually used by workers. Distinguish a control that exists on paper from one verified in operation.\n\n**Step 4 – Inspect and collect evidence**\n\nUse observation, interviews, equipment records, training records, permits, monitoring results and waste-transfer documentation as appropriate. Record the location, date, evidence and people responsible for follow-up. Protect confidential personnel and health information.\n\n**Step 5 – Prioritize corrective actions**\n\nAddress immediate threats first. For each action, define the required control, accountable owner, resources, due date and method of verification. Prefer eliminating or reducing the hazard at source over relying only on warnings or personal protective equipment.\n\n**Step 6 – Verify closure and review performance**\n\nReinspect the area and confirm that the action has reduced the risk or corrected the nonconformity. Review incidents, near misses, environmental results, overdue actions and recurring findings at the appropriate management meeting. Update procedures and training where the underlying process has changed.\n\n**Required outputs**\n\n1. Area-based hazard and environmental-aspect register.\n2. Register of applicable legal, permit, customer and internal obligations.\n3. Risk assessment and evidence of existing controls.\n4. Prioritized corrective-action plan with owners and due dates.\n5. Verification records and updated training or operating controls.","deep":"The exercise should test whether management controls are effective in the actual workplace. A fire extinguisher may be listed in an inspection record but inaccessible behind stored cartons; a chemical procedure may exist while incompatible containers are stored together. Environmental records may show waste collection without proving the receiving route is authorized. Use site observation and supporting evidence to verify each control. Prioritize immediate danger, then address systemic causes such as layout, maintenance, training, procurement or supervision. Keep legal conclusions tied to current local requirements and competent review.","applied":"Repeat the review after significant layout, process, chemical, equipment or regulatory changes. Use incident trends, inspection findings, worker feedback and environmental monitoring to select focus areas. Management should review overdue high-risk actions and verify that corrective measures remain effective, rather than closing findings solely because a document has been updated.","id":39,"diagram":"<figure class=\"lesson-diagram phase12-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 174\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Integrated Exercise: Managing Factory Safety, Environment and Compliance\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Integrated Exercise: Managing Factory Safety, Environment and Compliance</title><text x=\"20\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Integrated Exercise: Managing Factory Safety, Environment and Compliance</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Implementation Flow and Control Points</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Identify Risks</text><path d=\"M190 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"218\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"302\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Controls</text><path d=\"M388 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"416\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Audit</text><path d=\"M586 113 h20 m-7 -6 l7 6 -7 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><rect x=\"614\" y=\"78\" width=\"168\" height=\"70\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#eef4f8\" stroke=\"#52748a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Verification</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Record the owner, data, deadline and evidence in line with the factory procedure.</text></svg></figure>"}},"categories":{"Tổ chức và hệ thống quản trị":"Organization and Management Systems","Nhân sự và năng lực lao động":"Workforce and Labor Capability","Tài chính và chi phí nhà máy":"Factory Finance and Costs","Tài sản, an toàn và môi trường":"Assets, Safety and Environment","Điều hành và cải tiến":"Operations and Continuous Improvement","Thực hành tổng hợp":"Integrated Practice"}};
